  "query_preview": "..\nRest of Today and Sunday: An anomalous early summer negatively \ntilted trof is positioned over the northwestern US bringing\ncooler than normal conditions, scattered showers, a few\nthunderstorms, and breezy conditions to much of the Inland\nNorthwest. While the parent low is anchored near Portland, the\nsurface reflection can be clearly identified on radar as showers\nand thunderstorms twirl counter-clockwise around the low in \neastern Douglas County in central Washington. Daytime\ninstability has resulted in max CAPE of approximately 800 J/Kg\nwith precipitable water value of .7 to .8\". Despite the lower\nPWAT values, slow moving intense showers have produced locally\nheavy rain with radar estimating over an inch of rain today just\nnorthwest of Almira, WA along HWY2.  \n\nThe south and southeastern portion of the developing showers are\nmoving toward the Spokane metro area but are having difficulty\nmaintaining intensity as they encounter more stable air. As the\nsurface feature gradually meanders to the east, we anticipate\nsome shower activity will reach the Spokane-CdA corridor,\nalthough the most intense thunderstorms should remain just to\nthe north. The latest guidance still places downtown Spokane at\nless than 10% chance of thunderstorms this evening so we will be\nwatching closely and update as needed.  \n\nOvernight convection will wane with the loss of daytime heating\nalthough as we saw last night, a few scattered showers will\nlikely persist near the center of the circulation. The trof\nmoves farther inland Sunday resulting in a tightening pressure\ngradient across the Cascades. The cross-gradient winds will\nincrease through the morning hours and be quite gusty through\nnormal channeled terrain, gusting over 35 mph in the favored\nareas. The Columbia Basin will also increase southwest winds\nthrough the morning with gusts of 20-30 mph expected throughout\nthe basin.  \n\nMeanwhile, a deformation band is expected to develop and back\nitself across Idaho Sunday. This will result in lowering\nceilings and stratiform rain for much of the panhandle. \n\nMonday through Friday: Multiple bands of rain are expected to be\nthrown back around the low toward eastern WA and northern ID\nthrough Monday. There is poor agreement on the timing of rain,\nbut by the end of Monday, most of eastern WA and north ID should\nsee a wetting rain. By Tuesday, the trof over the western US\nreloads and deepens again, bringing more showers and isolated\nthunderstorms to the region and keeping temperatures about 10\ndegrees below normal for this time of year. It isn't until late\nin the week when there is some consensus that the trof weakens\nand moves inland, allowing for a weak ridge to build along the\ncoast. This will begin to nudge temperatures back up closer to\nnormal for this time of year (mid 70s to low 80s) just in time\nfor the 4th of July weekend.  \n\nBeyond next weekend, there is some model consensus that the\nwarm-up will continue beyond the 4th of July weekend with \ntemperatures reaching the 90s a few days later. /AB",
